! IF on a BRANCH TARGET -- Reference Manual p.32, class 2 of the three
! the page lists for Instruction Fetch: "The branch address of all
! successful branch operations."
!
! WEAKER STANDARD, hand-written from p.32, AND THAT IS NOW A NARROW
! CLAIM rather than a blanket one.  A deck oracle for the IF INDICATOR
! does exist -- jb_ax5b routine 1260 TEST 8C, transcribed here as
! ax5b_160_test8c.strap -- but every IF case in it is straight-line
! FALL-THROUGH into a below-LB region, which is p.32's CLASS 1.  None
! of them branches into a protected area.  So class 2, this block, has
! no deck test anywhere in the corpus (swept 2026-09-24), while class 1
! has a good one and is the next piece of work.
!
! THE RULE, p.32: "When an address alarm is caused by the location of
! the instruction which is to be executed next, indicator IF is turned
! on... If the branch address of a successful branch operation is such
! that control would pass into a protected area, the entire branch
! operation, including any counting, bit changing, or index alteration,
! is suppressed.  The address boundaries are ignored if the mask bit is
! zero."
!
! THE LAST SENTENCE IS AMBIGUOUS AND THE DECK SETTLES IT.  Read alone
! it could mean the indicator does not set when the mask bit is zero.
! It does set: TEST 8C case 3 is titled "ENABLE MASK 0 FETCH OK IND ON
! NO INT" and errors with "-INSTRUCT FETCH BELOW LB FAILS TO SET IF, NO
! INT, MASK 0, ENABLED."  The sentence attaches to the BRANCH
! SUPPRESSION before it.  This program runs the mask-ZERO case, so the
! indicator sets and the branch is NOT suppressed -- and the
! suppression is the mask-dependent half, which is not implemented.
!
! SO THIS BLOCK CHECKS THE INDICATOR AND DELIBERATELY CHECKS THAT THE
! BRANCH WAS TAKEN.  On a machine with the mask-dependent half the
! branch would still be taken here, because the mask bit is zero.
!
! A TARGET BELOW 32 IS AD'S BUSINESS.  p.45: "Branching to an address
! below 32 also actuates the indicator [AD]", and p.32 says an AD
! address "cannot actuate any of the address alarm indicators".  This
! model does not yet raise AD there, so such a branch raises NEITHER;
! the last block below pins that IF at least stays off, which is the
! half that is implemented.
!
! Instructions: BD, BE, LVI, SV, BB1, BBZ, BZB, B, BEW -- all
! implemented.

        SLC, (8)4100.0
START   BD,  $+.32                   ! disable: words 2 and 3 are protected
        LVI, $X13, TBL
        SV,  $X13, $IA
        LVI, $X13, FALLIN
        SV,  $X13, $LB               ! lower boundary := FALLIN
        LVI, $X13, ABOVE
        SV,  $X13, $UB               ! upper boundary := ABOVE
        BBZ, 3.57, $+1.0             ! control bit := 0: signal INSIDE
        BBZ, 12.21, $+1.0            ! IF's mask bit := 0
        BE,  $+.32                   ! monitoring is ineffective disabled
!
! ---- 1. CLASS 1: an instruction EXECUTED from inside the area ----
!   Control reaches PRE, which is OUTSIDE, and FALLS THROUGH into
!   FALLIN, which is the first word of the monitored area.  No branch
!   enters the area, so class 2 cannot contribute and this block is
!   class 1 alone.  It is the same arrangement routine 1260 TEST 8C
!   uses -- "SV,$X2,$LB" pointed at the label that follows, with
!   control falling straight through into it.
        B,   PRE
!
! ---- 2. and a BRANCH into the area ------------------------------
!   CLASS 2 CANNOT BE ISOLATED WHILE SUPPRESSION IS UNIMPLEMENTED,
!   and that is worth stating rather than pretending otherwise.  On
!   the real machine a protected branch TARGET suppresses the entire
!   branch operation, so control never arrives and only class 2 fires.
!   Here the branch is taken, the target executes, and class 1 fires
!   too -- same indicator, indistinguishable.  So this block witnesses
!   "IF is on after a branch into a protected area", which both
!   classes satisfy, and the class-2 signal's own injection now
!   SURVIVES.  It will separate when the mask-dependent half lands.
OUT2    BZB, $IF,  $                 ! hang if nothing lit IF
        BB,  $DF,  $                 ! hang if it lit 20 instead of 21
        BBZ, $IF,  $+1.0             ! clear for the next check
!
! ---- 3. an ordinary branch OUTSIDE the area lights nothing ------
        B,   $+.32                   ! taken, unmonitored target.  THE
                                     !   TARGET IS THE NEXT HALF-WORD: B is
                                     !   half-word format, so "$+1.0" would
                                     !   land on the second half of the
                                     !   full-word BB below.
        BB,  $IF,  $                 ! hang if an unmonitored branch lit IF
!
! ---- 4. a low BIT address is not a low BRANCH address ----------
!   ifSignal carries an "a >= 32" guard, because p.45 says "Branching
!   to an address below 32 also actuates the indicator [AD]" and p.32
!   says an AD address "cannot actuate any of the address alarm
!   indicators".  Without it a reference to the permanently protected
!   words 1-3 would raise IF where the machine raises AD.
!
!   THAT GUARD IS NOT WITNESSED BY THIS PROGRAM, and saying so is the
!   point of this block.  The injection that drops it SURVIVED: the
!   rows below touch word 3 as a BIT address, not as an instruction
!   location or a branch TARGET.  Witnessing it needs control actually
!   at words 1-3 -- the interval timer, the interruption address and
!   the boundary registers -- whose contents would then be executed as
!   instructions.  This model does not raise AD there either, so the
!   case is doubly unmodelled.
        BD,  $+.32                   ! disable first: word 3 is protected
        BB1, 3.0,  $+1.0             ! word 3 as a BIT address, not a target
        BE,  $+.32
        BB,  $IF,  $                 ! hang if that lit IF
        B,   PASS
PASS    BEW, $
!
! ---- the fall-through pair: PRE outside, FALLIN inside ----------
        SLC, (8)4277.40
PRE     NOP                          ! the last word BELOW the boundary
        SLC, (8)4300.0
FALLIN  B,   OUT2                    ! executed from INSIDE the area
        SLC, (8)4301.0               ! EXPLICIT: without it ABOVE lands at
                                     !   004300.40 -- the SECOND HALF of
                                     !   FALLIN's own word, because DD does
                                     !   not align after a half-word B.  UB
                                     !   would then equal LB and p.31's
                                     !   half-open area would be EMPTY, so
                                     !   nothing signals and this program
                                     !   passes for no reason at all.
ABOVE   DD(BU,64,8), 0               ! == the upper boundary, first word out
!
! ---- the interruption table --------------------------------------
!   IF's mask bit is zero, so nothing here should interrupt.  The
!   table exists because DS is permanently masked and a stray store
!   would otherwise run off into unloaded storage.
        SLC, (8)4500.0
TBL     DD, 0
        SLC, (8)4523.0
DSRTN   BB1, 6.0, $+1.0              ! entry 19 = DS
        END, START
